  "title": "CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION TO ANDHRA PRADESH",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<img alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/LEY3LW06Tte8DhhfII-K3WSa_Ktf5Zb5NVydfTfuehKWF6D4GbxbtMyFMtc3uJTBTONtD2LtLz114yNeYyXlqSecNTC0vUcgljPYqgut0Z5s48_sX3ehZlxMTtz29-gDo2h_smq4WKV011OAIKlDT9RhqHHNmiC_9Nf01EyDxf_WI8tCPXtGGF8U1HPRKdSQ?purpose=fullsize\" src=\"/books/Guide/Andhra_Pradesh CONSTABLE/images/image_24ynbwq.jpeg\"/>Andhra Pradesh is one of the 28 states of India, located on the southeastern coast of the Indian subcontinent. It is the seventh-largest state by area, covering approximately 1,62,975 square kilometres, and the tenth-most populous state with over 49 million inhabitants as per the 2011 Census. The state shares borders with Telangana and Chhattisgarh to the north, Odisha to the northeast, Tamil Nadu to the south, Karnataka to the west, and the Bay of Bengal to the east. Its coastline stretches for approximately 974 km, making it the state with the second-longest coastline in India after Gujarat.",
      "id": "bcpzxi0"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The state was reorganized on 2 June 2014 when the new state of Telangana was carved out of its northwestern districts. Before bifurcation, the combined state had Hyderabad as its capital. After bifurcation, Amaravati was designated as the proposed new capital of the residual Andhra Pradesh, though the capital question has remained a matter of political and administrative discussion in subsequent years.",
      "id": "voem5rc"
    },
    {
      "id": "3ozu4y3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.1 At a Glance – Key Facts"
